---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Kubernetes">
  <h1>Kubernetes</h1>
  <p>
    Identity is a single stateless container; the Kubernetes object set is
    minimal. This page is a reference manifest you can adapt — there is
    no Helm chart in the repo, intentionally.
  </p>

  <h2>Secret</h2>
  <p>The Postgres DSN, TOTP encryption key, per-project secrets key, and OAuth client secrets belong in a Secret:</p>
  <Code code={`apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: identity-secrets
  namespace: identity
type: Opaque
stringData:
  GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN: "postgres://identity:secret@postgres.identity.svc.cluster.local:5432/identity?sslmode=require"
  GATEWAY_TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY: "<base64 32-byte key>"
  # Required with postgres — encrypts per-project OAuth secrets at rest.
  # Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
  GATEWAY_PROJECT_SECRETS_KEY: "<base64 32-byte key>"
  GATEWAY_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET: "..."
  GATEWAY_OAUTH_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET: "..."`} lang="yaml" title="secret.yaml" />

  <p>
    The JWT signer defaults to <code>file</code> (<code>GATEWAY_JWT_SIGNER</code>),
    which reads the key ring JSON from the path in
    <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE</code>. Put the key ring in its own Secret and
    mount it as a volume (see the Deployment below):
  </p>
  <Code code={`apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: identity-jwt-keys
  namespace: identity
type: Opaque
stringData:
  jwt-keys.json: |
    [{"kid":"k-2026-05","private_key_pem":"-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n...\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n","active":true}]`} lang="yaml" title="jwt-keys-secret.yaml" />

  <h2>ConfigMap</h2>
  <Code code={`apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: identity-config
  namespace: identity
data:
  GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER: "postgres"
  GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE: "/etc/identity/jwt/jwt-keys.json"
  GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID: "proj_acme"
  GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_ID: "acme.example.com"
  GATEWAY_PASSKEY_ORIGIN: "https://acme.example.com"
  GATEWAY_TOTP_ISSUER: "Acme"
  GATEWAY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS: "https://acme.example.com"
  GATEWAY_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL: "true"
  GATEWAY_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID: "..."
  GATEWAY_OAUTH_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID: "..."`} lang="yaml" title="configmap.yaml" />

  <h2>Deployment</h2>
  <Code code={`apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: identity
  namespace: identity
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels: { app: identity }
  template:
    metadata:
      labels: { app: identity }
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: identity
          image: ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0
          ports:
            - { containerPort: 80, name: http }
            - { containerPort: 9090, name: metrics }
          envFrom:
            - configMapRef: { name: identity-config }
            - secretRef:    { name: identity-secrets }
          volumeMounts:
            - name: jwt-keys
              mountPath: /etc/identity/jwt
              readOnly: true
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: 80 }
            periodSeconds: 5
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: 80 }
            periodSeconds: 10
          resources:
            requests: { cpu: "100m", memory: "128Mi" }
            limits:   { cpu: "500m", memory: "256Mi" }
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
            runAsNonRoot: true
            capabilities:
              drop: ["ALL"]
      volumes:
        - name: jwt-keys
          secret:
            secretName: identity-jwt-keys`} lang="yaml" title="deployment.yaml" />

  <h2>Service</h2>
  <Code code={`apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: identity
  namespace: identity
  labels: { app: identity }
spec:
  selector: { app: identity }
  ports:
    - { name: http,    port: 80,   targetPort: http }
    - { name: metrics, port: 9090, targetPort: metrics }`} lang="yaml" title="service.yaml" />

  <h2>Scaling</h2>
  <p>
    The container is stateless — replicas are interchangeable. A single
    <code>Deployment</code> with 2-3 replicas behind a <code>Service</code>
    is sufficient for typical workloads. Vertical capacity is bounded by
    bcrypt: at cost 12, a single core can hash about 4 passwords per
    second. If signup rate is bursty, consider an HPA on CPU.
  </p>

  <h2>Pod Disruption Budget</h2>
  <Code code={`apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: identity
  namespace: identity
spec:
  minAvailable: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels: { app: identity }`} lang="yaml" />

  <h2>Ingress</h2>
  <p>
    Use whatever ingress the cluster has. The relevant facts:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Identity speaks HTTP/1.1 + HTTP/2 cleartext on <code>:80</code>.</li>
    <li>The path namespace is <code>/identity.v1.IdentityService/*</code> and <code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code> + <code>/health*</code>.</li>
    <li>Make sure the ingress preserves <code>Host</code> + <code>Origin</code> headers — Connect uses them, and CORS / passkey RP-ID checks depend on them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Bootstrap the first platform admin before exposing it</h2>
  <p>
    The control plane exposes <code>CreateFirstPlatformAdmin</code> — a one-time,
    trust-on-first-use bootstrap that creates the <strong>first</strong> platform
    operator while <code>platform_admins</code> is empty, then permanently closes
    once any admin exists. On a fresh, internet-reachable cluster an anonymous
    caller could win the first-admin race in the window before you create it, so
    <strong>bootstrap before you wire up the Ingress</strong> — while the pod is
    only reachable via <code>kubectl port-forward</code>:
  </p>
  <Code code={`kubectl -n identity port-forward svc/identity 8080:80 &
curl -sX POST http://localhost:8080/identity.v1.IdentityService/CreateFirstPlatformAdmin \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"ops@acme.example.com"}'
# → { "adminId": "...", "email": "ops@acme.example.com", "generatedPassword": "..." }`} lang="bash" title="bootstrap while port-forwarded" />
  <p>To also harden the RPC itself, add one of:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET</code> in the <code>identity-secrets</code>
      Secret — when set, the bootstrap requires the same
      <code>X-Admin-Secret</code> header as the other admin RPCs (a wrong or
      missing secret is <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>). TOFU stays zero-config
      only when no secret is configured.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_DISABLE_FIRST_ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP: "true"</code> in the
      <code>identity-config</code> ConfigMap — closes the RPC entirely
      (<code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>). With it closed, create the first admin
      by a direct insert into <code>platform_admins</code>, or toggle the flag
      off just long enough to bootstrap and back on (no first-party seed CLI
      ships).
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
